            "text": "In the quiet hours before dawn, when most of the world is asleep, I find myself thinking about how words shape the spaces inside our heads. It is strange to imagine that black marks on white paper, or glowing pixels on a screen, can move us to tears or laughter, can change the course of a day, can make us think differently about ourselves and each other.\n\nWhen I stare at a blank page, there is a sense of openness that is both terrifying and liberating. The possibilities are endless, and yet something in me wants to choose a line, a path, a thread to follow. Maybe today it will be a memory of walking by the sea, the way the salt clung to my skin and the wind tangling my hair. Maybe tomorrow it will be the sound of rain on a tin roof in a town I've never visited.\n\nWhat I love about writing is how it allows me to try on different lives, to imagine perspectives that are not my own, and to connect with someone I will never meet across time and space. Somewhere, someone might read this and picture me at this moment, and for a second, we are in the same room. The magic is in that small bridge. I think that's why I keep coming back to this practice, to find those tiny moments of recognition. And because it feels like breathing, necessary in order to feel alive. There is something sacred here."
          }
        ],
